          "vendorSlug": "datadog",
          "section": "3.1",
          "title": "Secure API Keys",
          "description": "- API keys authorize data ingestion and programmatic access, so a leaked key lets an attacker submit false telemetry or pull organizational data - Purpose-specific, descriptively named keys let you revoke a single compromised integration without breaking everything else - Storing keys in a secret manager and keeping them out of source code prevents the most common leak vector — credentials committed to repositories - Unused or orphaned keys are standing credentials an attacker can exploit undetected",
