          "vendorSlug": "datadog",
          "section": "3.2",
          "title": "Secure Application Keys",
          "description": "- Application keys inherit the full permissions of the user who created them, so a leaked key grants an attacker that user's entire level of access - Scoping keys to the minimum required permissions limits what a compromised key can read or change - Regular rotation shortens the lifespan of any key that is exposed before the leak is detected - Tying a privileged user's app key to a broad scope effectively turns a key leak into an account takeover",
